Rc<RefCell<T>> is for single-threaded shared ownership: multiple owners in one thread, with runtime borrow checking via RefCell. Use it when you need shared mutable state but don't need thread safety.

Arc<Mutex<T>> is for multi-threaded shared ownership: atomic reference counting plus a mutex for exclusive mutable access. Use it when data must be shared across threads.

Example Rc<RefCell<T>>:
```rust
use std::rc::Rc;
use std::cell::RefCell;

let shared = Rc::new(RefCell::new(5));
let a = Rc::clone(&shared);
let b = Rc::clone(&shared);
*a.borrow_mut() += 1;
println!("{}", b.borrow());
```

Example Arc<Mutex<T>>:
```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::thread;

let shared = Arc::new(Mutex::new(5));
let mut handles = vec![];
for _ in 0..2 {
    let s = Arc::clone(&shared);
    handles.push(thread::spawn(move || {
        *s.lock().unwrap() += 1;
    }));
}
for h in handles { h.join().unwrap(); }
println!("{}", *shared.lock().unwrap());
```

Key difference: Rc/RefCell is fast but single-threaded; Arc/Mutex is thread-safe but has synchronization overhead.

